Origin: EmberBSD (AI-assisted). Build these consumers with the one Ruby, Go,
Lua, MySQL client, FFmpeg, PCRE, Qt, GCC and OpenJDK version in the export
instead of an old version.
Umbrello 26.08 builds with Qt 6 and KDE Frameworks 6 through BUILD_WITH_QT6, and
then suffixes its programs and data directory with 6 instead of 5.
ZoneMinder 1.38 already looks for PCRE2, so with the old PCRE it lost regex
support silently.
VLC's Lua scripts used math.pow and assigned to a for-loop variable; Lua 5.5
allows neither, so the scripts compute the power with ^ and use new locals.
OpenSceneGraph's own Lua finders stop at Lua 5.2, so it gets the library and
headers directly; its Lua plugin needs no source change. Its buildlink3.mk
now passes the default Lua, not Lua 5.2, to the packages that use it.
vifm linked the system Lua but compiled against its bundled Lua 5.4 headers;
it now drops the bundled copy and uses the headers of the Lua it links.
HexPatch built a bundled Lua 5.4 through mlua's vendored feature although the
recipe pulled in the system Lua; it now uses the system Lua 5.5.
ntopng compiled against its bundled Lua 5.4 headers, and Lua 5.5 adds a seed
argument to lua_newstate and rejects assignments to for-loop variables in its
scripts; ntopng now uses the system Lua 5.5 headers and copies those loop
variables to locals.
Yazi's crates enable mlua's vendored feature by default, so Yazi built its
own Lua 5.5 and never used the system Lua the recipe pulled in; it now links
the system Lua.
ET: Legacy asks its own Lua finder for Lua 5.4, and that finder could not read
the Lua 5.5 version numbers; it now reads them, and the recipes point it at the
default Lua.
Xournal++ finds Lua through CMake's FindLua, which knows Lua 5.5, and its
plugin API needs no change; it now depends on the default Lua.
The Haskell Lua bindings set the collector parameters with Lua 5.4 commands
that Lua 5.5 replaces with LUA_GCPARAM, and Lua 5.5 counts LUA_GCSTEP in
bytes; a small C wrapper now translates the commands, so the hslua packages
and Pandoc keep their API on the default Lua.
darktable's Lua finder rejects Lua 5.5, and darktable then builds without Lua
support because it does not fall back to its in-tree Lua; the finder now
accepts Lua 5.5, which darktable's Lua API builds and runs with.
The Powder Toy builds its Lua 5.2 mode, which uses the current Lua API, with
Lua 5.5 once the integer API casts and the module registration that Lua 5.3
dropped are provided and its LuaSocket helper loads without module(); it no
longer depends on Lua 5.1.
gitk only runs wish, and the default Tk is Tk 9.1, so it no longer needs
pkgsrc's separate Tk 9.0.
Flycast's LuaBridge bindings build and run with Lua 5.5, so it finds the
default Lua through pkg-config instead of Lua 5.2.
Emacs 29 and 30 compile native Lisp through the libgccjit API, which GCC 16
still provides, so they use GCC 16's libgccjit like Emacs 31 does.
s6-networking's openssl option already builds its libtls TLS support with
libretls on OpenSSL, the one TLS library in the export, so the libressl option,
which built the same libtls support on LibreSSL, is gone.
NetBSD on i386, x86_64 and aarch64 defaulted Java packages to OpenJDK 17;
OpenJDK 21 runs there too, so they now default to it. Without OpenJDK 17 in
the export, those packages silently lost their JDK dependency.
lua-compat53's C API header stopped at Lua 5.4, and Lua 5.5 no longer defines
lua_assert, so it accepts Lua 5.5 and defines lua_assert itself; its tests
pass with Lua 5.5.
KStars finds OpenCV 5 by its split calibration modules, calib and geometry,
instead of OpenCV 4's calib3d.
frei0r's face detection plugins include and link OpenCV 5's xobjdetect module
from opencv-contrib, which now has CascadeClassifier.
digiKam no longer includes the C API headers that OpenCV 5 removed, and takes
the ml module from opencv-contrib, which replaces opencv-contrib-face.
lua-bit32 builds from the same lua-compat53 0.10 distfile and patches as
lua-compat53, which build with Lua 5.5; its tests pass with Lua 5.5.

